{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"RESIDUE is a pure post-fixpoint annotation over the unique grounded labeling, so it adds no new iteration and preserves determinism and polynomial cost; it leaves IN/OUT/SUS labels and root validity unchanged, so the skeptical fixed point and its guarantees are intact.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"Seeding initial labels from bounded defense chains can violate the monotonicity guarantee the target relies on: a node may be seeded IN because it has a defense chain of length <= d, but later in the fixpoint that defense can be defeated by an attacker outside the seeded chain, requiring the node to flip to OUT; this non-monotone flip breaks the claim that seeding is conservative and can corrupt the standard skeptical fixed point. Example graph: A attacks B; B attacks C; C attacks D; D attacks A. With d=2, B is seeded IN via the defense chain B<-D (length 2) if D is initially assumed IN, but in the true grounded labeling D is OUT (attacked by C, which is IN because B is OUT), so B must become OUT—seeding flipped IN to OUT and changed the computed extension.","counterexample":["A attacks B","B attacks C","C attacks D","D attacks A","seed depth d=2","seeded: B=IN via chain B<-D","grounded: C=IN, D=OUT, B=OUT, A=OUT","non-monotone flip: B seeded IN then fixed to OUT"],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":false,"case":"For d at least the longest defense chain in the polynomial-sized graph, the monotone seeding computes exactly the standard grounded extension, preserving determinism, polynomial cost, and root validity while making reinstatement chains explicit.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}